---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Matrix from '../components/Matrix.astro';
---
<Base
  title="How it works — EdgeShaping"
  description="How EdgeShaping detects AI bots, classifies them by purpose, and compares them with human traffic through the AHTG and AHQG matrices."
  lang="en"
  path="/how-it-works"
  altPath="/ja/how-it-works"
>
  <section class="deep" style="padding-top: 160px;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--ai);">How it works</span>
      <h2 style="color: var(--foam);">Detect. Classify. Compare.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        EdgeShaping answers one simple question:<br />
        <strong style="color: var(--foam);">Which AI bot is reading which page, when — and why?</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shallow">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="tag">Step — Detect</span>
          <h3>Dictionary-based detection</h3>
          <p>
            A continuously updated crawler dictionary identifies traffic from the ChatGPT, Claude,
            Perplexity and Gemini families and beyond, logging bot name, URL, timestamp and user
            agent. The dictionary grows with every update.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="tag">Step — Classify</span>
          <h3>Grouped by purpose</h3>
          <p>
            Not every "AI bot" means the same thing. Training crawls feed models. Search &amp; RAG
            fetches back answers. User-triggered fetches happen because a person just asked an AI
            about you. EdgeShaping reports each visit by what it was for.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="tag">Step — Compare</span>
          <h3>Side by side with humans</h3>
          <p>
            AI visits are matched against human page views (AHTG) or Google Search clicks (AHQG),
            turning the gap between your two audiences into a four-quadrant map, per page.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="deep-2">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow reveal" style="color: var(--ai);">AHTG / AHQG Matrix</span>
      <h2 class="reveal" style="color: var(--foam);">Four quadrants to read.</h2>
      <p class="lead reveal" style="margin-bottom: 48px;">
        The AHTG matrix plots AI visits against human page views; the AHQG matrix plots them
        against Google Search clicks. Different human-side data, same way of reading the map.
      </p>
      <div class="reveal"><Matrix lang="en" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shallow-2">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow reveal">Beyond the matrix</span>
      <h2 class="reveal">Two observations past the quadrants.</h2>
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top: 40px;">
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="tag">Inferred Path</span>
          <h3>URLs the AI assumed would exist</h3>
          <p>
            URLs that AI bots request even though they're not in your sitemap. Some are stale
            links. Others are paths an AI invented because it expected your site to have that
            page — which makes them a signal of demand you haven't written yet.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
          <span class="tag">Dark Pages</span>
          <h3>Pages nobody reaches</h3>
          <p>
            Pages that exist in your sitemap but are reached by neither AI nor humans. For
            important pages, that's a linking problem. For the rest, it's a merge, delete or
            noindex decision waiting to be made.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top: 48px;" class="reveal">
        <a class="btn btn-ai" href="/editions">Compare editions</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
